1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,650
A practical word before the break, so that the lab does not surprise you.

2
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:08,950
In this session's lab you will implement gradient descent yourself.

3
00:00:08,950 --> 00:00:17,600
You will compute the gradient using automatic differentiation, which you met in Session two, and you will write the weight update as a line of code.

4
00:00:17,600 --> 00:00:22,600
There is no model object, no training-loop helper, no convenient one-liner.

5
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:34,150
That is deliberate, and I want to say so out loud, because when you open the notebook you will notice that the code is longer than it needs to be and you may think you have been given the hard version by accident.

6
00:00:34,150 --> 00:00:39,075
The reason is that today is the only time you will see the machinery with the lid off.

7
00:00:39,075 --> 00:00:47,850
From Session eight you will use the library's model class and its optimiser, and they will do every one of those steps for you, correctly and invisibly.

8
00:00:47,850 --> 00:00:56,200
If you have never once watched a gradient being computed and applied, those libraries are magic, and when they go wrong you have nothing to think with.

9
00:00:56,200 --> 00:00:59,750
So: today you write it; from next week you call it.

10
00:01:00,350 --> 00:01:01,750
Block two.

11
00:01:01,750 --> 00:01:04,850
What one weighted sum can and cannot do.

12
00:01:04,850 --> 00:01:14,625
On the page, this block is the sections "What one weighted sum can represent", "What one weighted sum can never represent: XOR" and "Why a hidden layer helps".

13
00:01:14,625 --> 00:01:20,400
This block is the famous part of the story, and it is short on biology and long on logic.

14
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:28,025
We are going to find the exact boundary of what the model from Block one can represent, hit it hard, and then see what fixes it.

15
00:01:28,025 --> 00:01:34,375
The fix is the hidden layer, and it is the thing that makes everything in Sessions eight to twelve possible.

16
00:01:34,975 --> 00:01:40,125
Before asking what a perceptron cannot do, let us be fair and see what it can.

17
00:01:40,125 --> 00:01:46,500
The simplest possible test problems are the logic gates, with two inputs that are each zero or one.

18
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:51,325
Four possible inputs, which we can draw as the four corners of a square.

19
00:01:51,325 --> 00:01:55,525
Take AND, which should output one only when both inputs are one.

20
00:01:55,525 --> 00:02:03,200
Choose the weights by hand: w one equals one, w two equals one, and the bias equals minus one point five.

21
00:02:03,200 --> 00:02:05,625
No training, just chosen.

22
00:02:05,625 --> 00:02:08,100
Now check all four corners.

23
00:02:08,100 --> 00:02:14,450
At zero, zero: z is nought plus nought minus one point five, which is minus one point five.

24
00:02:14,450 --> 00:02:17,375
Negative, so the output is zero.

25
00:02:17,375 --> 00:02:18,550
Correct.

26
00:02:18,550 --> 00:02:24,850
At zero, one: z is nought plus one minus one point five, which is minus nought point five.

27
00:02:24,850 --> 00:02:27,300
Negative, output zero.

28
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:28,475
Correct.

29
00:02:28,475 --> 00:02:33,700
At one, zero: the same by symmetry, minus nought point five, output zero.

30
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:34,875
Correct.

31
00:02:34,875 --> 00:02:41,125
And at one, one: z is one plus one minus one point five, which is plus nought point five.

32
00:02:41,125 --> 00:02:43,425
Positive, output one.

33
00:02:43,425 --> 00:02:44,600
Correct.

34
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:49,575
That is the AND truth table, exactly, from one weighted sum and a step.

35
00:02:49,575 --> 00:02:53,375
And look at the picture, because the geometry is the point.

36
00:02:53,375 --> 00:03:02,575
The line z equals zero runs across the square, and it has cut off exactly one corner, the one-one corner, leaving the other three on the far side.

37
00:03:02,575 --> 00:03:05,650
The perceptron is not really doing logic.

38
00:03:05,650 --> 00:03:09,375
It is drawing a line and asking which side you are on.

39
00:03:09,975 --> 00:03:17,900
Your turn, and this one is worth pausing the video for, because you will understand the next five minutes much better if you have tried it yourself.

40
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:22,900
Find w one, w two and b for a perceptron that implements OR.

41
00:03:22,900 --> 00:03:29,825
OR outputs one when at least one input is one, so it is one at three corners and zero only at zero, zero.

42
00:03:29,825 --> 00:03:31,350
Two hints.

43
00:03:31,350 --> 00:03:38,275
First, think geometrically rather than algebraically: where does the line have to sit relative to the four corners?

44
00:03:38,275 --> 00:03:41,525
For AND, the line cut off the one-one corner.

45
00:03:41,525 --> 00:03:46,150
For OR, which corner do you need to cut off, and on which side should it be?

46
00:03:46,150 --> 00:03:50,525
Second hint: start from the AND weights and ask what you need to change.

47
00:03:50,525 --> 00:03:53,025
The slope or the intercept?

48
00:03:53,025 --> 00:03:59,100
And a third question when you have an answer: is your solution the only one that works, or are there others?

49
00:03:59,100 --> 00:04:04,600
That question matters more than it looks, and the slide after next answers it with a number.

50
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:06,150
Pause here.

51
00:04:06,750 --> 00:04:09,400
Here are the three gates together.

52
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:15,700
AND, with weights one, one and bias minus one point five: the line cuts off the one-one corner.

53
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:19,825
OR, with weights one, one and bias minus nought point five.

54
00:04:19,825 --> 00:04:26,150
The slope is identical to AND; only the bias changed, which slides the line without turning it.

55
00:04:26,150 --> 00:04:32,350
Now it cuts off the zero-zero corner instead, and the other three corners are on the positive side.

56
00:04:32,350 --> 00:04:37,500
If your answer had a different bias between nought and minus one, it also works.

57
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:44,600
And NAND, which is AND with every sign flipped: weights minus one, minus one, bias plus one point five.

58
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:51,925
Flipping all the signs flips which side of the line is positive, so NAND is the exact opposite of AND at every corner.

59
00:04:51,925 --> 00:04:57,450
Under each corner on the slide is the value of z, so you can check all twelve by eye.

60
00:04:57,450 --> 00:05:02,400
And to the question of whether your solution was unique: no, not remotely.

61
00:05:02,400 --> 00:05:14,350
The notebook searches a grid of possible weights and finds roughly five thousand combinations that implement AND, about nine and a half thousand for OR and about four and a half thousand for NAND.

62
00:05:14,350 --> 00:05:18,300
There is nothing special about the particular numbers I chose.

63
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:21,275
Any line in the right place does the job.

64
00:05:21,875 --> 00:05:25,275
Now XOR, and this is where it stops working.

65
00:05:25,275 --> 00:05:30,075
XOR outputs one when the inputs differ, and zero when they are the same.

66
00:05:30,075 --> 00:05:32,825
So: zero, zero gives zero.

67
00:05:32,825 --> 00:05:35,050
Zero, one gives one.

68
00:05:35,050 --> 00:05:37,300
One, zero gives one.

69
00:05:37,300 --> 00:05:39,525
One, one gives zero.

70
00:05:39,525 --> 00:05:42,125
Look at where those sit on the square.

71
00:05:42,125 --> 00:05:48,675
The two corners that should output one are zero-one and one-zero, which are diagonally opposite each other.

72
00:05:48,675 --> 00:05:55,675
The two corners that should output zero are zero-zero and one-one, which are also diagonally opposite each other.

73
00:05:55,675 --> 00:06:03,025
So you need a straight line with both ends of one diagonal on one side, and both ends of the other diagonal on the other side.

74
00:06:03,025 --> 00:06:04,450
Try it.

75
00:06:04,450 --> 00:06:08,025
Genuinely, take a moment and try to draw that line.

76
00:06:08,025 --> 00:06:10,775
Any line, at any angle, anywhere.

77
00:06:10,775 --> 00:06:18,950
You cannot do it, and once you have tried for thirty seconds the reason becomes obvious: the two diagonals cross in the middle of the square.

78
00:06:18,950 --> 00:06:26,500
Anything you separate with a straight cut keeps neighbours together, and in XOR the corners that belong together are not neighbours.

79
00:06:26,500 --> 00:06:31,975
The slide shows three failed attempts, each getting three corners right and one wrong.

80
00:06:31,975 --> 00:06:47,800
There is also a short algebraic proof on the course page, which I will not do out loud, but the shape of it is this: write down the four inequalities that the four rows of the truth table demand, add two of them together, and you get a statement that directly contradicts a third.

81
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:50,100
So no weights exist.

82
00:06:50,100 --> 00:06:53,450
Not none that we found: none that can exist.

83
00:06:54,050 --> 00:06:58,925
In case the argument did not convince you, here is the brute-force version.

84
00:06:59,525 --> 00:07:14,375
The notebook takes every value of w one, w two and b from minus three to plus three in steps of nought point one, which is two hundred and twenty-six thousand, nine hundred and eighty-one combinations, and checks each one against each truth table.

85
00:07:14,975 --> 00:07:17,750
Thousands of them implement AND.

86
00:07:17,750 --> 00:07:20,325
Thousands implement OR.

87
00:07:20,925 --> 00:07:23,375
Thousands implement NAND.

88
00:07:23,375 --> 00:07:26,075
Not one implements XOR.

89
00:07:26,075 --> 00:07:30,125
Not a single one out of two hundred and twenty-six thousand.

90
00:07:30,125 --> 00:07:33,075
And that is not bad luck with the grid.

91
00:07:33,075 --> 00:07:37,475
It is what the proof says: the set of weights that would work is empty.

92
00:07:38,075 --> 00:07:42,200
So that is the model being unable to represent XOR.

93
00:07:42,200 --> 00:07:47,050
What happens if we ignore that and train it anyway with Block one's machinery?

94
00:07:47,050 --> 00:07:53,975
This is worth seeing, because it is how such a failure actually looks in practice, and you will meet the pattern again.

95
00:07:53,975 --> 00:07:59,875
Train a linear model with a sigmoid on XOR, with gradient descent, for as long as you like.

96
00:07:59,875 --> 00:08:01,875
It does not crash.

97
00:08:01,875 --> 00:08:04,225
It does not produce an error.

98
00:08:04,225 --> 00:08:09,975
It settles down, converges nicely, and predicts nought point five for all four inputs.

99
00:08:09,975 --> 00:08:13,150
Nought point five means "I have no idea".

100
00:08:13,150 --> 00:08:22,750
The model has found the least-wrong compromise available to it: since any line it draws gets at least one corner wrong, the safest thing is to sit on the fence everywhere.

101
00:08:22,750 --> 00:08:25,200
And you can see it in the loss.

102
00:08:25,200 --> 00:08:30,875
The final loss is nought point six nine three one, which is the natural logarithm of two.

103
00:08:30,875 --> 00:08:35,125
That is exactly the loss you get from predicting a half for everything.

104
00:08:35,125 --> 00:08:37,675
Not approximately: exactly.

105
00:08:37,675 --> 00:08:48,975
Compare the same training on AND, which the model can represent: it reaches predictions of nought point zero zero three, nought point one one three, nought point one one three and nought point eight four.

106
00:08:48,975 --> 00:08:51,225
Confident, and correct.

107
00:08:51,225 --> 00:08:56,850
Here is the practical lesson, and it is a lesson about debugging, not about XOR.

108
00:08:56,850 --> 00:09:06,450
When a model trains without complaint and produces a flat, uncertain output, the first thing to suspect is not the learning rate or the amount of data.

109
00:09:06,450 --> 00:09:10,600
Suspect that the model cannot represent the thing you are asking for.

110
00:09:11,200 --> 00:09:15,650
The fix is almost embarrassingly simple once you have seen the picture.

111
00:09:15,650 --> 00:09:17,950
One line cannot do it.

112
00:09:17,950 --> 00:09:19,875
Two lines can.

113
00:09:19,875 --> 00:09:23,300
Think about what each of our gates does geometrically.

114
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:29,525
OR cuts off the zero-zero corner, so it is true everywhere except the bottom-left.

115
00:09:29,525 --> 00:09:34,750
NAND cuts off the one-one corner, so it is true everywhere except the top-right.

116
00:09:34,750 --> 00:09:37,550
Now ask where both are true at once.

117
00:09:37,550 --> 00:09:41,375
Everywhere except the bottom-left and except the top-right.

118
00:09:41,375 --> 00:09:46,050
That is exactly the two corners we want, zero-one and one-zero.

119
00:09:46,650 --> 00:09:48,875
That is XOR.

120
00:09:48,875 --> 00:09:51,525
So: compute OR from the inputs.

121
00:09:51,525 --> 00:09:54,325
Compute NAND from the same inputs.

122
00:09:54,325 --> 00:09:57,175
Then take the AND of those two results.

123
00:09:57,175 --> 00:10:00,075
The picture on the slide makes it visual.

124
00:10:00,675 --> 00:10:05,925
Each of the two gates draws its own line, and the two lines are roughly parallel.

125
00:10:05,925 --> 00:10:12,575
The region where both are satisfied is the band between them, and the two corners we want are sitting in that band.

126
00:10:12,575 --> 00:10:16,350
One straight line cannot carve out a band; two can.

127
00:10:16,350 --> 00:10:20,575
That band is the first non-linear decision boundary in this course.

128
00:10:20,575 --> 00:10:24,700
It is made of straight pieces, and it is not itself straight.

129
00:10:25,300 --> 00:10:34,350
And here is that arrangement drawn as a network, which is the first time in this course you are looking at a real neural network rather than a single unit.

130
00:10:34,350 --> 00:10:36,875
There are two inputs at the bottom.

131
00:10:36,875 --> 00:10:45,050
They both go to two units in the middle, which we call the hidden layer, because its outputs are not what the network reports: they are internal.

132
00:10:45,050 --> 00:10:52,000
Hidden unit one has weights plus one and plus one, and a bias of minus nought point five: that is OR.

133
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:59,075
Hidden unit two has weights minus one and minus one, and a bias of plus one point five: that is NAND.

134
00:10:59,075 --> 00:11:07,675
Then the two hidden units feed one output unit, with weights plus one and plus one and a bias of minus one point five: that is AND.

135
00:11:07,675 --> 00:11:16,025
Every single unit is the same thing we have had since the first slide of this session: a weighted sum, plus a bias, then an activation.

136
00:11:16,025 --> 00:11:18,725
Nothing new has been introduced.

137
00:11:18,725 --> 00:11:24,675
What is new is only that some units take their input from other units instead of from the data.

138
00:11:24,675 --> 00:11:27,775
And notice that nothing here was trained.

139
00:11:27,775 --> 00:11:31,950
I chose all nine numbers by hand from gates we already knew.

140
00:11:31,950 --> 00:11:40,525
In a moment we will let gradient descent find its own, but the point is that the architecture is what makes XOR possible, not the training.

141
00:11:41,125 --> 00:11:46,775
Let us push all four inputs through and check it, because it is worth doing once by hand.

142
00:11:46,775 --> 00:11:50,875
Take one, one, which is the case people find least obvious.

143
00:11:50,875 --> 00:11:53,000
Both inputs are on.

144
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:56,450
OR fires, so hidden unit one outputs one.

145
00:11:56,450 --> 00:12:03,425
NAND does not fire, because NAND is off precisely when both inputs are on, so hidden unit two outputs zero.

146
00:12:04,025 --> 00:12:13,200
The output unit therefore sees one and zero: its weighted sum is one plus nought minus one point five, which is negative, so it outputs zero.

147
00:12:13,200 --> 00:12:16,825
And XOR of one and one is indeed zero.

148
00:12:16,825 --> 00:12:18,000
Correct.

149
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:20,000
Take zero, one.

150
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:22,775
OR fires, so hidden one is one.

151
00:12:23,375 --> 00:12:28,550
NAND also fires, because not both inputs are on, so hidden two is one.

152
00:12:28,550 --> 00:12:35,375
The output unit sees one and one: one plus one minus one point five is positive, so it outputs one.

153
00:12:35,375 --> 00:12:38,525
And XOR of zero and one is one.

154
00:12:38,525 --> 00:12:39,700
Correct.

155
00:12:39,700 --> 00:12:43,250
Run all four and you get zero, one, one, zero.

156
00:12:43,850 --> 00:12:46,500
That is XOR, exactly.

157
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:56,325
Written compactly, the whole network is two lines: H equals step of X W one plus b one, and y equals step of H W two plus b two.

158
00:12:56,325 --> 00:13:01,875
A matrix product, an activation, another matrix product, another activation.

159
00:13:02,475 --> 00:13:08,700
Every network in the rest of this course is that pattern, repeated more times with bigger matrices.

160
00:13:08,700 --> 00:13:25,625
And when the notebook trains this architecture properly, with tanh in the hidden layer and a sigmoid on the output instead of hard steps, gradient descent finds its own weights and predicts nought point zero zero one, nought point nine nine eight, nought point nine nine eight, and nought point zero zero one.

161
00:13:25,625 --> 00:13:31,025
It rediscovers a solution of this shape without being told about OR or NAND.

162
00:13:31,625 --> 00:13:37,925
Now an experiment that looks like a technicality and is actually the most important point in the block.

163
00:13:37,925 --> 00:13:42,700
Take that same two-layer network and remove the activation functions.

164
00:13:42,700 --> 00:13:45,350
Just the weighted sums, stacked.

165
00:13:45,350 --> 00:13:47,725
Write out what it computes.

166
00:13:47,725 --> 00:13:51,300
The hidden layer gives W one x plus b one.

167
00:13:51,300 --> 00:13:58,425
The output layer takes that and gives W two, times the quantity W one x plus b one, plus b two.

168
00:13:58,425 --> 00:14:05,625
Multiply it out, and you get W two W one, times x, plus the quantity W two b one plus b two.

169
00:14:05,625 --> 00:14:07,725
Look at that result.

170
00:14:07,725 --> 00:14:11,125
W two W one is just some matrix.

171
00:14:11,125 --> 00:14:15,100
W two b one plus b two is just some vector.

172
00:14:15,100 --> 00:14:20,300
So the whole two-layer network is a single weighted sum with different numbers in it.

173
00:14:20,300 --> 00:14:22,575
It is picture one again.

174
00:14:22,575 --> 00:14:24,700
And that is general.

175
00:14:24,700 --> 00:14:30,325
Any number of linear layers stacked on top of each other collapses into one linear layer.

176
00:14:30,325 --> 00:14:35,200
A hundred layers without activations is exactly as powerful as one.

177
00:14:35,200 --> 00:14:46,950
The notebook confirms it on XOR: remove the tanh and the two-layer network goes straight back to predicting a half everywhere, with a loss of log two, exactly as the single linear model did.

178
00:14:46,950 --> 00:14:50,425
So it is not the depth that gives a network its power.

179
00:14:50,425 --> 00:14:54,000
It is the non-linear activation between the layers.

180
00:14:54,000 --> 00:14:59,925
The weighted sums draw straight boundaries, and the activation is what lets the next layer bend them.

181
00:14:59,925 --> 00:15:03,600
Remove it and all the depth in the world buys you nothing.

182
00:15:03,600 --> 00:15:08,025
If you remember one technical fact from this session, make it this one.

183
00:15:08,625 --> 00:15:13,650
A short practical slide, which will save you time in every lab from here on.

184
00:15:13,650 --> 00:15:22,200
When you build a network in code, most of your errors will be shape errors: a matrix of the wrong size meeting a matrix of another wrong size.

185
00:15:22,200 --> 00:15:25,375
The error messages are not always helpful.

186
00:15:25,375 --> 00:15:31,100
The cure is to be able to say, for every arrow in the diagram, what shape the numbers have.

187
00:15:31,100 --> 00:15:36,675
Session one gave you the rule: for a matrix product, the inner dimensions must match.

188
00:15:36,675 --> 00:15:41,450
On this slide, the shapes of the XOR network are written along each arrow.

189
00:15:41,450 --> 00:15:45,400
The input is a batch of examples, each with two numbers.

190
00:15:45,400 --> 00:15:50,750
The first weight matrix is two by two, taking two inputs to two hidden units.

191
00:15:50,750 --> 00:15:53,925
The hidden activations are a batch by two.

192
00:15:53,925 --> 00:15:57,000
The second weight matrix is two by one.

193
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,575
The output is a batch by one.

194
00:15:59,575 --> 00:16:05,975
Get into the habit of printing the shape of a tensor whenever you are unsure, rather than reasoning about it.

195
00:16:05,975 --> 00:16:09,925
In the lab, this picture is the training loop, line for line.

196
00:16:10,525 --> 00:16:16,775
To close Block two, the history, because this particular piece of mathematics had consequences.

197
00:16:16,775 --> 00:16:32,275
In nineteen sixty-nine, Marvin Minsky and Seymour Papert published a book called Perceptrons, in which they proved exactly what we showed by picture this morning: a single-layer perceptron cannot represent XOR, or anything else that is not linearly separable.

198
00:16:32,275 --> 00:16:34,875
The mathematics was correct.

199
00:16:34,875 --> 00:16:37,450
What followed is more debatable.

200
00:16:37,450 --> 00:16:47,100
The book is widely credited with contributing to the first so-called AI winter: funding moved away from neural networks, and the field went quiet for years.

201
00:16:47,100 --> 00:16:49,475
Now, the interesting part.

202
00:16:49,475 --> 00:16:57,125
It was already known that multi-layer networks are more powerful, because you have just seen that yourself with nine hand-chosen numbers.

203
00:16:57,125 --> 00:17:00,675
The missing piece was not the idea of a hidden layer.

204
00:17:00,675 --> 00:17:03,500
It was a practical way to train one.

205
00:17:03,500 --> 00:17:10,500
Choosing weights by hand works for XOR, with two inputs and three units, and is hopeless for anything real.

206
00:17:10,500 --> 00:17:18,725
The method that solved it is backpropagation, which became widely known in the mid-nineteen eighties and which is Session nine's subject.

207
00:17:18,725 --> 00:17:28,075
So the gap between knowing that hidden layers help and being able to use them was roughly fifteen years, and it was a gap in training, not in architecture.

208
00:17:28,075 --> 00:17:35,450
You will see the same shape of story in Session ten, where convolutional networks waited two decades for data and hardware.

209
00:17:35,450 --> 00:17:38,575
A correct idea is not enough on its own.

210
00:17:39,175 --> 00:17:40,650
Block three.

211
00:17:40,650 --> 00:17:43,900
Biology, and why representation matters.

212
00:17:43,900 --> 00:17:50,975
On the page, this block is the sections "Biological analogues" and "Representation matters as much as the model".

213
00:17:50,975 --> 00:17:53,700
Blocks one and two were about models.

214
00:17:53,700 --> 00:18:03,850
This block is about inputs, and its claim is one that I think is the most useful thing in this session: what you feed a model matters at least as much as which model you choose.

215
00:18:03,850 --> 00:18:11,225
We will see it four times, in four different decades, on four different problems, and it will be the same lesson every time.

216
00:18:11,225 --> 00:18:20,075
We will also meet the biological problem that the rest of the session, the lab and your capstone project are built on: predicting secondary structure.

217
00:18:20,675 --> 00:18:24,325
Let us start by showing that XOR is not a toy.

218
00:18:24,325 --> 00:18:30,525
It is a real shape of problem in molecular biology, and here it is in protein-DNA binding.

219
00:18:30,525 --> 00:18:44,150
In two thousand and two, Bulyk and colleagues used protein-binding microarrays to measure how strongly the zinc-finger protein Zif two six eight, and mutants of it, bound to all sixty-four possible central triplets of DNA.

220
00:18:44,150 --> 00:18:46,975
So this is not a model of binding.

221
00:18:46,975 --> 00:18:50,175
It is a measurement of all the combinations.

222
00:18:50,175 --> 00:18:55,400
What they found is that the effect of one position depends on what is at the others.

223
00:18:55,400 --> 00:18:59,900
A base that improves binding in one context makes it worse in another.

224
00:18:59,900 --> 00:19:04,875
The slide shows a toy version of that, stripped down so you can see the logic.

225
00:19:04,875 --> 00:19:13,900
Imagine a site of two positions, where binding is strong when one position is a purine and the other is a pyrimidine, and weak when they are the same kind.

226
00:19:13,900 --> 00:19:16,600
There are sixteen possible sites.

227
00:19:16,600 --> 00:19:21,275
Now look at panel b, the sequence logo built from the strong sites.

228
00:19:21,275 --> 00:19:25,800
At each position, each base appears in two of the eight strong sites.

229
00:19:25,800 --> 00:19:27,975
Equal frequencies.

230
00:19:27,975 --> 00:19:31,250
The information content is zero bits.

231
00:19:31,250 --> 00:19:35,450
The logo is completely flat, and it tells you nothing at all.

232
00:19:35,450 --> 00:19:39,775
And yet panel c shows the pair determines binding completely.

233
00:19:39,775 --> 00:19:44,675
The information is there; it is simply not in either position separately.

234
00:19:44,675 --> 00:19:46,900
That is XOR.

235
00:19:46,900 --> 00:19:55,375
Same structure, different costume: the answer depends on whether the two positions agree or differ, and no per-position score can express it.

236
00:19:55,375 --> 00:20:05,450
And so a position weight matrix, which is a sum of per-position scores, and a consensus sequence, which is even cruder, both fail here, however carefully you build them.

237
00:20:05,450 --> 00:20:08,075
Not because your counts were bad.

238
00:20:08,075 --> 00:20:12,550
Because a sum of independent terms cannot express a dependency.

239
00:20:13,150 --> 00:20:19,050
Now, Block two's answer to XOR was to make the model more powerful: add a hidden layer.

240
00:20:19,050 --> 00:20:23,700
There is a second answer, and it is the one this block is really about.

241
00:20:23,700 --> 00:20:27,050
Leave the model linear, and change the input.

242
00:20:27,050 --> 00:20:34,425
Take XOR, and give the model a third input: x one times x two, the product of the two original inputs.

243
00:20:34,425 --> 00:20:37,300
Now a plain weighted sum can do it.

244
00:20:37,300 --> 00:20:43,200
The weights one, one and minus two, with a bias of zero, reproduce XOR exactly.

245
00:20:43,200 --> 00:20:50,375
Check it: at one, one, the sum is one plus one minus two times one, which is zero, so the output is zero.

246
00:20:50,375 --> 00:20:55,125
At one, zero, the product is zero, so the sum is one, output one.

247
00:20:55,125 --> 00:20:56,825
And so on.

248
00:20:56,825 --> 00:20:59,425
The model did not change at all.

249
00:20:59,425 --> 00:21:02,975
It is still one weighted sum, still picture one.

250
00:21:02,975 --> 00:21:07,950
We changed what we fed it, and an impossible problem became a trivial one.

251
00:21:07,950 --> 00:21:11,250
So why is this not the universal solution?

252
00:21:11,250 --> 00:21:18,450
Because of one word in what I just said: someone had to know that the product of the two inputs was the useful thing to add.

253
00:21:18,450 --> 00:21:23,325
For XOR that is easy, because the problem is tiny and we can see it.

254
00:21:23,325 --> 00:21:30,850
For real binding sites, or for secondary structure, nobody knows in advance which combinations of which positions matter.

255
00:21:30,850 --> 00:21:39,075
If you have thirteen residues of sequence, there are an enormous number of possible interaction terms, and almost all of them are useless.

256
00:21:39,075 --> 00:21:42,525
That is precisely what a hidden layer buys you.

257
00:21:42,525 --> 00:21:49,575
A hidden unit computes some combination of the inputs, and training decides which combinations are worth computing.

258
00:21:49,575 --> 00:21:54,500
You are not supplying the features; you are supplying the capacity to find them.

259
00:21:54,500 --> 00:21:57,075
Keep both halves of this slide.

260
00:21:57,075 --> 00:21:59,850
Better model, or better input.

261
00:21:59,850 --> 00:22:05,575
The rest of this block is the same choice, played out in the history of a real prediction problem.

